{"author":{"id":"609bf866d4a5d716c19faf5d617b3bf3de478230d29602d72aad5aa90a80466f"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1794307446,"nonce":"XyXdjhAS5F0dkUfh6E9pkiJeV/c=","title":"A missing primitive in a required library is not refused at load, and jit fails to compile the function that names it","message":"HashLink refuses to start when a program names a primitive its loaded HDLL lacks and the library is required (module.c, hl_module_init_natives: Failed to load function lib@name), whether or not the primitive is ever called. Ash does not: with @:hlNative(\"sdl\", \"no_such_prim\") and an sdl.hdll lacking it, interp and hybrid start and run lazily (the call fails only if reached, with a non-catchable Error), and jit stops with 'JIT tier 0 failed to compile findex N' before the program starts, without naming the library or the function. Observed on macOS with the arm64 sdl.hdll from examples/heaps_base2d and a Haxe program calling nothing missing.\n\nDecide which behaviour is wanted. Refusing at load like HashLink is the parity answer and names the primitive up front; keeping the lazy path needs the call to raise a catchable error and jit to compile a raising stub as it now does for optional libraries (6b8e1a0). Either way the jit message should name the native. Related: GitHub issue 1.","files":null}]}